    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "Numbers are the foundation of mathematics and daily communication. Cardinal numbers — one, two, three, and so on — express quantity, while ordinal numbers — first, second, third — express position or order. English numbers follow patterns: the teens (thirteen through nineteen) add '-teen' to single digits, and the tens (twenty, thirty, forty) add '-ty'. Large numbers use commas as separators: 1,000 (one thousand), 1,000,000 (one million), 1,000,000,000 (one billion). Note that British and American English historically differed on 'billion' — in modern usage, both mean a thousand million. Decimals use a point (3.14), not a comma as in many other languages. Fractions have special vocabulary: a half (½), a third (⅓), a quarter (¼), a fifth (⅕). 'Nought' or 'zero' represents 0, while 'nil' is used in sports scores and 'oh' in phone numbers. Percentages, ratios, and approximations ('about', 'roughly', 'approximately', 'nearly') are essential for everyday discussion. Counting systems extend to dozens (12), scores (20), and gross (144). Roman numerals (I, V, X, L, C, D, M) still appear on clocks, book chapters, and formal documents. Understanding how English handles numbers — from basic arithmetic to phone numbers, addresses, and statistics — is crucial for academic, professional, and daily life.",
